<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/handshake.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/handshake-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/handshake.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="799" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/claim-handbook/">Claim handbook</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Appraiser, Public Adjuster, or Attorney: Which Fits Your Claim Problem?</span></nav><p class="kicker">Handbook · 7 min read · October 20, 2026</p><h1>Appraiser, Public Adjuster, or Attorney: Which Fits Your Claim Problem?</h1><p class="lede">A practical comparison of appraisers, public adjusters, and attorneys, and how to match each one to the kind of claim problem you have.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Three professionals, three different jobs</h2>
<p>When a property claim stalls, policyholders hear a lot of advice: hire an appraiser, call a public adjuster, get a lawyer. All three can help, but they do different things, get paid differently, and fit different problems. Picking the wrong one can waste time and money. This guide lays out what each does so you can match the help to the problem.</p>
<p>A note up front: North Dakota Insurance Appraiser is an appraisal practice. We are not a law firm and not a public adjuster, so we have tried to describe the other two roles fairly and generally.</p>

<h2>The appraiser</h2>
<h3>What they do</h3>
<p>An appraiser works within the appraisal clause of your policy. Once appraisal is demanded, each side names an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and the panel sets the amount of loss. Your appraiser inspects the property, builds an independent estimate, and negotiates with the other appraiser. Appraisers also do estimate reviews outside formal appraisal, helping you understand where an insurer’s numbers fall short.</p>
<h3>Best fit</h3>
<ul>
<li>The insurer agrees the damage is covered.</li>
<li>The dispute is about quantities, prices, scope, or matching.</li>
<li>You want a resolution that is usually faster and less expensive than litigation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Limits</h3>
<p>Appraisers decide value, not coverage. They do not give legal advice, cannot sue, and cannot address bad-faith or claim-handling issues. Most policies require the appraiser to be disinterested, so fee structures tied to the size of the award can raise challenges.</p>

<h2>The public adjuster</h2>
<h3>What they do</h3>
<p>A public adjuster is a licensed professional who represents the policyholder in preparing, presenting, and negotiating the claim with the insurer. They often handle the claim from early on: documenting damage, preparing the estimate and inventory, and dealing with the adjuster. Public adjusters are licensed and regulated by the state; check with the North Dakota Insurance Department to confirm any license.</p>
<h3>Best fit</h3>
<ul>
<li>A large or complex loss where you need help managing the whole claim.</li>
<li>Extensive contents inventories or business income calculations.</li>
<li>You do not have the time or experience to handle the paperwork.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Limits</h3>
<p>Public adjusters commonly work for a percentage of the claim payment, and the terms are set by contract and regulation. They do not give legal advice. Because they are advocates, a public adjuster may not qualify as a disinterested appraiser on the same claim under many policies.</p>

<h2>The attorney</h2>
<h3>What they do</h3>
<p>An attorney licensed in North Dakota can advise you on what your policy means, whether the insurer’s position is lawful, and what remedies exist. They can negotiate, file suit, and pursue claims such as breach of contract. North Dakota recognizes a common-law bad-faith claim against insurers, and its insurance code addresses unfair claim settlement practices; only an attorney can tell you whether those apply to your situation.</p>
<h3>Best fit</h3>
<ul>
<li>The insurer denied the claim or a large part of it on coverage grounds.</li>
<li>You received a reservation of rights letter or an accusation of misrepresentation.</li>
<li>A suit-limitation deadline is approaching.</li>
<li>You believe the claim was handled unfairly or in bad faith.</li>
<li>The insurer refuses to honor an appraisal award.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Limits</h3>
<p>Litigation can take longer and cost more than appraisal. Many attorneys also rely on appraisers or estimators to establish the dollar value of the loss.</p>

<h2>Matching the problem to the help</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>“They agree it’s covered but paid too little.”</strong> Start with an estimate review, then consider appraisal.</li>
<li><strong>“I can’t keep up with the claim at all.”</strong> A public adjuster may fit, depending on the size of the loss.</li>
<li><strong>“They denied it” or “they say it’s excluded.”</strong> Talk to a North Dakota attorney.</li>
<li><strong>“They won’t return calls and keep moving the goalposts.”</strong> Consider a complaint to the North Dakota Insurance Department, and talk to an attorney about your options.</li>
<li><strong>“A deadline is close.”</strong> Call an attorney first. Suit-limitation periods come from your policy and state law.</li>
</ol>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Worth knowing</b> These roles can overlap. An attorney might ask an appraiser to handle the valuation piece through appraisal while reserving legal claims. A policyholder who used a public adjuster early might later need an attorney for a coverage fight. The question is which problem you are solving right now.</div>

<h2>Questions to ask anyone you hire</h2>
<ul>
<li>How are you paid, and is it in writing?</li>
<li>Do you have any relationship with my insurer?</li>
<li>What part of my problem can you solve, and what part can’t you?</li>
<li>Who will actually inspect the property and handle my file?</li>
<li>How do you communicate updates, and how often?</li>
<li>If you are licensed, can I verify that license?</li>
</ul>
<p>Be cautious of anyone who promises a specific result or a dollar figure before reviewing your policy and the damage. Honest professionals in all three roles talk about likely ranges and risks, not guarantees.</p>

<h2>A word on timing</h2>
<p>Whoever you hire, bring them in before positions harden. An appraiser reviewing an estimate early can flag missing items while they are still easy to photograph. An attorney consulted early can spot deadline and coverage problems before they become urgent. Waiting until the last week of a deadline narrows everyone’s options.</p>

<h2>The North Dakota angle</h2>
<p>After a major hail or wind event, out-of-area contractors and claim representatives sometimes arrive in North Dakota towns within days. Take time to check credentials and read contracts before signing anything. Storm-season pressure is real, but so are the costs of a bad fit.</p>
<p>If your problem is a dispute over the amount of a covered loss, North Dakota Insurance Appraiser can review the estimate and discuss whether appraisal makes sense. Learn more on our <a href="/appraisal-representation/">appraisal representation</a> page. If your problem is legal, we will tell you so and suggest you speak with a North Dakota attorney.</p>
